    shortDescriptionEn: "A standardised document describing an ML model: its purpose, quality metrics, limitations, training data, and ethical considerations. Required by FCA SS1/23 and recommended by the Alan Turing Institute.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "The Model Card is a concept proposed by Google (Mitchell et al., 2019) that has become an industry standard for documenting ML models, and a core tool of Responsible AI.\n\n**Required sections of a Model Card:**\n\n**1. Model Details:**\n- Model type, architecture, version\n- Training date, owner, contacts\n- Licence and usage restrictions\n\n**2. Intended Use:**\n- Primary intended uses (what it was built for)\n- Out-of-scope uses (what it is NOT intended for)\n- Target audience\n\n**3. Factors:**\n- Relevant factors: demographic, technical conditions\n- Evaluation factors: which groups/conditions were evaluated\n\n**4. Metrics:**\n- Performance metrics (accuracy, AUC, F1, RMSE)\n- Decision thresholds and their justification\n- Variation across groups (disaggregated evaluation)\n\n**5. Evaluation Data:**\n- Datasets used for evaluation\n- Preprocessing and the rationale for choices made\n\n**6. Training Data:**\n- (where possible) a description of the training data\n- Sources and collection procedures\n\n**7. Ethical Considerations:**\n- Biases and their mitigation\n- Privacy considerations\n- Potential harms\n\n**UK regulatory context:**\n- **FCA SS1/23:** requires model documentation for models used in financial decisions\n- **ICO Guidance on AI:** documentation as part of accountability (GDPR Article 5(2))\n- **Alan Turing Institute:** recommends Model Cards as part of an AI assurance framework\n\n**Tools:**\n- Google Model Card Toolkit (Python)\n- Hugging Face Model Hub: a standard form for all public models\n- IBM AI FactSheets",
