    slug: "duration-matching",
    termUk: "Узгодження дюрації для пенсійного ALM",
    termEn: "Duration Matching for Pension ALM",
    category: "data",
    shortDescription: "An asset-liability management (ALM) technique that aligns the interest-rate sensitivity (duration) of a pension scheme's assets with that of its liabilities, immunising the funding position against yield-curve movements.",
    fullDescription: "Duration matching is a cornerstone of asset-liability management (ALM) for UK defined benefit (DB) pension schemes. Pension liabilities are long-dated cash flows discounted at market interest rates; their present value is therefore highly sensitive to rate changes. Duration measures this sensitivity (the percentage change in value for a 1% parallel shift in yields). By holding assets — typically gilts and interest-rate swaps — whose duration matches the liabilities, a scheme immunises its funding ratio against the dominant risk factor.\n\nKey concepts: Macaulay duration (weighted-average time to cash flows), modified duration (price sensitivity), and convexity (second-order sensitivity capturing curvature). Full immunisation often requires matching key-rate durations across multiple points on the yield curve, not just a single aggregate figure, because liability cash flows span 40+ years.\n\nWhere ML helps: machine learning enhances duration matching by (1) forecasting cash-flow profiles using survival and longevity models on member data, (2) optimising hedge portfolios under transaction-cost and collateral constraints via reinforcement learning, and (3) stress-testing the match against non-parallel yield-curve shifts using generative scenario models. The September 2022 LDI crisis, when gilt yields spiked and collateral calls cascaded, demonstrated why robust, ML-informed liquidity stress-testing of duration-matched hedges is now mandatory under TPR guidance.\n\nUK context: schemes work with the Pensions Regulator (TPR) funding code, advised by actuarial firms (Mercer, Aon, WTW). Codeworth builds ALM analytics that combine actuarial cash-flow projection with ML-driven scenario generation for trustee dashboards.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "An asset-liability management (ALM) technique that aligns the interest-rate sensitivity (duration) of a pension scheme's assets with that of its liabilities, immunising the funding position against yield-curve movements.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "Duration matching is a cornerstone of asset-liability management (ALM) for UK defined benefit (DB) pension schemes. Pension liabilities are long-dated cash flows discounted at market interest rates; their present value is therefore highly sensitive to rate changes. Duration measures this sensitivity (the percentage change in value for a 1% parallel shift in yields). By holding assets — typically gilts and interest-rate swaps — whose duration matches the liabilities, a scheme immunises its funding ratio against the dominant risk factor.\n\nKey concepts: Macaulay duration (weighted-average time to cash flows), modified duration (price sensitivity), and convexity (second-order sensitivity capturing curvature). Full immunisation often requires matching key-rate durations across multiple points on the yield curve, not just a single aggregate figure, because liability cash flows span 40+ years.\n\nWhere ML helps: machine learning enhances duration matching by (1) forecasting cash-flow profiles using survival and longevity models on member data, (2) optimising hedge portfolios under transaction-cost and collateral constraints via reinforcement learning, and (3) stress-testing the match against non-parallel yield-curve shifts using generative scenario models. The September 2022 LDI crisis, when gilt yields spiked and collateral calls cascaded, demonstrated why robust, ML-informed liquidity stress-testing of duration-matched hedges is now mandatory under TPR guidance.\n\nUK context: schemes work with the Pensions Regulator (TPR) funding code, advised by actuarial firms (Mercer, Aon, WTW). Codeworth builds ALM analytics that combine actuarial cash-flow projection with ML-driven scenario generation for trustee dashboards.",
