    slug: "longevity-swap-ml",
    termUk: "ML-ціноутворення свопів довголіття",
    termEn: "ML Longevity Swap Pricing",
    category: "data",
    shortDescription: "Machine learning approaches to pricing and hedging longevity swaps — instruments that transfer the risk of pension members living longer than expected from a scheme to a reinsurer.",
    fullDescription: "A longevity swap hedges longevity risk: the scheme pays a fixed (expected) stream of pension cash flows and receives the actual realised cash flows, so if members live longer than projected the counterparty (typically a reinsurer) bears the cost. For large UK DB schemes, longevity is one of the few remaining unhedged risks once rates and inflation are addressed via LDI, and multi-billion-pound longevity swaps are now common.\n\nPricing challenge: longevity swaps depend on projecting future mortality improvement decades ahead. Traditional actuarial models (CMI projection model, Lee-Carter) extrapolate mortality trends, but these are sensitive to base-table choice, improvement assumptions, and basis risk (the mismatch between a scheme's members and any index used).\n\nWhere ML helps: machine learning improves longevity pricing through (1) richer mortality modelling that incorporates socioeconomic, postcode, and benefit-amount features to capture the well-documented mortality gradient by affluence, (2) neural and Gaussian-process extensions of stochastic mortality models for better-calibrated uncertainty, (3) scenario generation for pandemic and medical-breakthrough tail risks, and (4) quantifying basis risk between scheme experience and population indices (used in index-based hedges).\n\nUK context: reinsurers (RGA, SCOR, Pacific Life Re, Munich Re) and the Continuous Mortality Investigation (CMI) underpin the market. Codeworth builds longevity analytics that blend CMI-consistent actuarial models with ML-derived member-level mortality signals for trustee and insurer pricing.",
