    slug: "causal-forest",
    termUk: "Причинний ліс (causal forest)",
    termEn: "Causal Forest",
    category: "ai",
    shortDescription: "A machine learning method based on random forests that estimates heterogeneous treatment effects — how the causal impact of an intervention varies across individuals or segments.",
    fullDescription: "A causal forest (Wager and Athey, 2018; part of the generalized random forest family) estimates the conditional average treatment effect (CATE) — the causal effect of a treatment as a function of an individual's features. Where a classic A/B test reports a single average effect, a causal forest answers 'for whom does this work best, and for whom does it backfire?', enabling targeted, personalised interventions.\n\nHow it differs from a standard random forest: instead of splitting trees to predict an outcome, a causal forest splits to maximise the heterogeneity of the treatment effect across leaves, and uses 'honest' estimation (separate data subsamples for choosing splits and for estimating effects within leaves) to obtain valid confidence intervals. It typically operates on data from a randomised experiment or after adjusting for confounders, often combined with propensity scoring (doubly robust / orthogonalised estimation).\n\nBusiness value: heterogeneous treatment effects unlock uplift modelling — targeting a marketing offer, retention call, or price discount only at customers it will actually move, rather than everyone. This both improves ROI and avoids wasting incentives on 'sure things' or 'lost causes'.\n\nUK context: under FCA Consumer Duty, firms must avoid foreseeable harm; causal forests help demonstrate that an intervention (e.g., a financial promotion) benefits the targeted segment rather than exploiting vulnerable customers. Codeworth uses causal forests (via EconML and grf) for uplift modelling in marketing, retention, and pricing while documenting fairness across customer segments.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "A machine learning method based on random forests that estimates heterogeneous treatment effects — how the causal impact of an intervention varies across individuals or segments.",
