    slug: "mediation-analysis-ml",
    termUk: "ML-аналіз медіації",
    termEn: "ML Mediation Analysis",
    category: "ai",
    shortDescription: "A causal technique that decomposes the effect of a treatment into a direct effect and an indirect effect transmitted through one or more mediating variables, increasingly powered by machine learning estimators.",
    fullDescription: "Mediation analysis asks not just whether X affects Y, but how — by separating the total effect into a direct effect (X to Y, holding the mediator fixed) and an indirect effect (X to mediator M to Y). In Pearl's causal framework these are formalised as the natural direct effect (NDE) and natural indirect effect (NIE), defined via nested counterfactuals and identifiable under explicit assumptions encoded in a DAG.\n\nWhy ML matters here: classic mediation (Baron-Kenny regression) assumes linear, interaction-free relationships. Modern ML mediation uses flexible estimators (gradient boosting, random forests, neural networks) for the outcome and mediator models, combined with doubly robust / debiased machine learning so that the effect estimates remain valid even when the nuisance models are complex. This handles non-linearities and high-dimensional confounders that linear mediation cannot.\n\nBusiness and policy use: mediation reveals mechanisms. Does a training programme raise productivity directly, or via improved confidence (the mediator)? Does a price cut increase sales directly, or by increasing site traffic? Understanding the pathway guides where to invest and supports causal storytelling to stakeholders.\n\nUK context: mediation is central to fairness auditing — decomposing how a sensitive attribute (e.g., postcode as a proxy for ethnicity) influences a credit decision directly versus through legitimate financial mediators, which is exactly the analysis the FCA and ICO expect for non-discrimination evidence. Codeworth applies debiased ML mediation to fairness audits and mechanism analysis for marketing and HR analytics.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "A causal technique that decomposes the effect of a treatment into a direct effect and an indirect effect transmitted through one or more mediating variables, increasingly powered by machine learning estimators.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "Mediation analysis asks not just whether X affects Y, but how — by separating the total effect into a direct effect (X to Y, holding the mediator fixed) and an indirect effect (X to mediator M to Y). In Pearl's causal framework these are formalised as the natural direct effect (NDE) and natural indirect effect (NIE), defined via nested counterfactuals and identifiable under explicit assumptions encoded in a DAG.\n\nWhy ML matters here: classic mediation (Baron-Kenny regression) assumes linear, interaction-free relationships. Modern ML mediation uses flexible estimators (gradient boosting, random forests, neural networks) for the outcome and mediator models, combined with doubly robust / debiased machine learning so that the effect estimates remain valid even when the nuisance models are complex. This handles non-linearities and high-dimensional confounders that linear mediation cannot.\n\nBusiness and policy use: mediation reveals mechanisms. Does a training programme raise productivity directly, or via improved confidence (the mediator)? Does a price cut increase sales directly, or by increasing site traffic? Understanding the pathway guides where to invest and supports causal storytelling to stakeholders.\n\nUK context: mediation is central to fairness auditing — decomposing how a sensitive attribute (e.g., postcode as a proxy for ethnicity) influences a credit decision directly versus through legitimate financial mediators, which is exactly the analysis the FCA and ICO expect for non-discrimination evidence. Codeworth applies debiased ML mediation to fairness audits and mechanism analysis for marketing and HR analytics.",
