    slug: "shap-waterfall",
    termUk: "SHAP водоспад (waterfall) пояснюваність",
    termEn: "SHAP Waterfall Explainability",
    category: "ai",
    shortDescription: "An explainability visualisation that uses SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values to show, for a single prediction, how each feature pushes the model output up or down from the baseline to the final value.",
    fullDescription: "A SHAP waterfall plot explains an individual model prediction by decomposing it into additive feature contributions grounded in cooperative game theory. SHAP (Lundberg and Lee, 2017) assigns each feature a Shapley value — its fair share of the prediction relative to a baseline (the expected model output). The waterfall plot starts at this baseline (E[f(x)]) and stacks each feature's positive (red, pushing the prediction higher) or negative (blue, pushing it lower) contribution until it reaches the final prediction f(x).\n\nWhy it is valued: SHAP has strong theoretical guarantees — local accuracy (contributions sum exactly to the prediction), missingness, and consistency — which is why it has become the de facto standard for instance-level explanations. TreeSHAP computes exact values efficiently for tree ensembles (XGBoost, LightGBM, random forests), while KernelSHAP and DeepSHAP handle arbitrary models.\n\nGlobal vs local: a single waterfall explains one decision (local), while aggregating SHAP values across many instances (beeswarm and bar summary plots) reveals global feature importance and direction — bridging individual transparency and overall model understanding.\n\nUK context: SHAP waterfalls are the practical workhorse for regulatory and customer-facing explainability. Under UK GDPR Article 22 and the FCA's expectations, individuals affected by automated decisions (credit, insurance pricing) may require a meaningful explanation; a SHAP waterfall provides an auditable, per-decision rationale ('your application was declined chiefly because of recent missed payments and high credit utilisation'). Codeworth integrates SHAP waterfalls into model-decision logs and adverse-action notices for regulated clients.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "An explainability visualisation that uses SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values to show, for a single prediction, how each feature pushes the model output up or down from the baseline to the final value.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "A SHAP waterfall plot explains an individual model prediction by decomposing it into additive feature contributions grounded in cooperative game theory. SHAP (Lundberg and Lee, 2017) assigns each feature a Shapley value — its fair share of the prediction relative to a baseline (the expected model output). The waterfall plot starts at this baseline (E[f(x)]) and stacks each feature's positive (red, pushing the prediction higher) or negative (blue, pushing it lower) contribution until it reaches the final prediction f(x).\n\nWhy it is valued: SHAP has strong theoretical guarantees — local accuracy (contributions sum exactly to the prediction), missingness, and consistency — which is why it has become the de facto standard for instance-level explanations. TreeSHAP computes exact values efficiently for tree ensembles (XGBoost, LightGBM, random forests), while KernelSHAP and DeepSHAP handle arbitrary models.\n\nGlobal vs local: a single waterfall explains one decision (local), while aggregating SHAP values across many instances (beeswarm and bar summary plots) reveals global feature importance and direction — bridging individual transparency and overall model understanding.\n\nUK context: SHAP waterfalls are the practical workhorse for regulatory and customer-facing explainability. Under UK GDPR Article 22 and the FCA's expectations, individuals affected by automated decisions (credit, insurance pricing) may require a meaningful explanation; a SHAP waterfall provides an auditable, per-decision rationale ('your application was declined chiefly because of recent missed payments and high credit utilisation'). Codeworth integrates SHAP waterfalls into model-decision logs and adverse-action notices for regulated clients.",
