    slug: "fca-consumer-duty-ml",
    termUk: "FCA Consumer Duty та ML",
    termEn: "FCA Consumer Duty and Machine Learning",
    category: "regulation",
    shortDescription: "How the FCA's Consumer Duty (in force from July 2023) shapes the design, validation, and monitoring of machine learning systems used by UK financial firms to ensure good outcomes and avoid foreseeable harm.",
    fullDescription: "The FCA Consumer Duty, which came into force for new and existing products on 31 July 2023, sets a higher and clearer standard of consumer protection across UK financial services. It comprises an overarching Principle (a firm must act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers), three cross-cutting rules (act in good faith, avoid foreseeable harm, enable customers to pursue their financial objectives), and four outcomes (products and services, price and fair value, consumer understanding, consumer support).\n\nImplications for ML: any model that influences pricing, targeting, eligibility, communications, or support is now squarely within scope. Firms must evidence that (1) pricing/uplift models deliver fair value and do not exploit inertia or vulnerability (e.g., no algorithmic 'price walking'), (2) targeting and recommendation models do not steer vulnerable customers toward harmful products, (3) automated communications are clear and tested for understanding, and (4) outcomes are monitored across customer segments to detect disparate harm.\n\nGovernance requirements: the Duty demands outcomes monitoring with data, board-level accountability, and the ability to identify and act on poor outcomes for groups with characteristics of vulnerability. This translates into ML obligations: fairness testing across segments, explainability (e.g., SHAP) for individual decisions, drift and outcome monitoring, and documented challenge of model logic.\n\nUK context: Codeworth helps firms operationalise Consumer Duty in ML by building outcomes dashboards (segment-level pricing fairness, vulnerability flags), fairness-audit pipelines, and explainability logs that withstand FCA scrutiny.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "How the FCA's Consumer Duty (in force from July 2023) shapes the design, validation, and monitoring of machine learning systems used by UK financial firms to ensure good outcomes and avoid foreseeable harm.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "The FCA Consumer Duty, which came into force for new and existing products on 31 July 2023, sets a higher and clearer standard of consumer protection across UK financial services. It comprises an overarching Principle (a firm must act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers), three cross-cutting rules (act in good faith, avoid foreseeable harm, enable customers to pursue their financial objectives), and four outcomes (products and services, price and fair value, consumer understanding, consumer support).\\n\\nImplications for ML: any model that influences pricing, targeting, eligibility, communications, or support is now squarely within scope. Firms must evidence that (1) pricing/uplift models deliver fair value and do not exploit inertia or vulnerability (e.g., no algorithmic 'price walking'), (2) targeting and recommendation models do not steer vulnerable customers toward harmful products, (3) automated communications are clear and tested for understanding, and (4) outcomes are monitored across customer segments to detect disparate harm.\\n\\nGovernance requirements: the Duty demands outcomes monitoring with data, board-level accountability, and the ability to identify and act on poor outcomes for groups with characteristics of vulnerability. This translates into ML obligations: fairness testing across segments, explainability (e.g., SHAP) for individual decisions, drift and outcome monitoring, and documented challenge of model logic.\\n\\nUK context: Codeworth helps firms operationalise Consumer Duty in ML by building outcomes dashboards (segment-level pricing fairness, vulnerability flags), fairness-audit pipelines, and explainability logs that withstand FCA scrutiny.",
    exampleEn: "An insurer's renewal-pricing model is audited under Consumer Duty: segment-level monitoring detects that long-tenured customers face higher margins (a price-walking signal), so the firm caps the loyalty penalty and documents the fair-value remediation for the FCA.",
