    slug: "uk-national-ai-strategy",
    termUk: "Національна стратегія ШІ Великої Британії (2021)",
    termEn: "UK National AI Strategy (2021)",
    category: "regulation",
    shortDescription: "The UK government's ten-year strategy, published in September 2021, setting out plans to invest in the AI ecosystem, support AI adoption across the economy, and govern AI effectively.",
    fullDescription: "The UK National AI Strategy, published on 22 September 2021 by the (then) Office for Artificial Intelligence, DCMS, and BEIS, set a ten-year vision to make the UK a global AI superpower. It is organised around three pillars: (1) investing in the long-term needs of the AI ecosystem (skills, research, compute, data, and talent — including routes such as the Alan Turing Institute and AI scholarships), (2) ensuring AI benefits all sectors and regions of the economy (adoption beyond the tech hubs, supporting SMEs and public services), and (3) governing AI effectively (a pro-innovation, context-based regulatory approach rather than a single AI statute).\n\nHow it evolved: the Strategy was followed by the 2022 AI Action Plan, the March 2023 AI Regulation White Paper (establishing five cross-sector principles — safety/security/robustness, transparency/explainability, fairness, accountability/governance, contestability/redress — implemented by existing regulators rather than a new central body), the November 2023 Bletchley Park AI Safety Summit, and the creation of the AI Safety Institute (AISI). This trajectory distinguishes the UK's lighter-touch, regulator-led model from the EU AI Act's centralised, risk-tiered legislation.\n\nWhy it matters for business: the Strategy signals where UK funding, skills programmes, and procurement preferences flow, and frames the principles-based regime that firms must satisfy through their sector regulators (FCA, ICO, MHRA, Ofcom).\n\nUK context: Codeworth aligns client AI governance with the five cross-sector principles and the relevant regulator's expectations, positioning projects to access UK innovation funding and public-sector procurement.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "The UK government's ten-year strategy, published in September 2021, setting out plans to invest in the AI ecosystem, support AI adoption across the economy, and govern AI effectively.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "The UK National AI Strategy, published on 22 September 2021 by the (then) Office for Artificial Intelligence, DCMS, and BEIS, set a ten-year vision to make the UK a global AI superpower. It is organised around three pillars: (1) investing in the long-term needs of the AI ecosystem (skills, research, compute, data, and talent — including routes such as the Alan Turing Institute and AI scholarships), (2) ensuring AI benefits all sectors and regions of the economy (adoption beyond the tech hubs, supporting SMEs and public services), and (3) governing AI effectively (a pro-innovation, context-based regulatory approach rather than a single AI statute).\\n\\nHow it evolved: the Strategy was followed by the 2022 AI Action Plan, the March 2023 AI Regulation White Paper (establishing five cross-sector principles — safety/security/robustness, transparency/explainability, fairness, accountability/governance, contestability/redress — implemented by existing regulators rather than a new central body), the November 2023 Bletchley Park AI Safety Summit, and the creation of the AI Safety Institute (AISI). This trajectory distinguishes the UK's lighter-touch, regulator-led model from the EU AI Act's centralised, risk-tiered legislation.\\n\\nWhy it matters for business: the Strategy signals where UK funding, skills programmes, and procurement preferences flow, and frames the principles-based regime that firms must satisfy through their sector regulators (FCA, ICO, MHRA, Ofcom).\\n\\nUK context: Codeworth aligns client AI governance with the five cross-sector principles and the relevant regulator's expectations, positioning projects to access UK innovation funding and public-sector procurement.",
