    slug: "pose-estimation-ml",
    termUk: "Оцінка пози (MediaPipe/OpenPose)",
    termEn: "Pose Estimation (MediaPipe/OpenPose)",
    category: "ai",
    shortDescription: "A computer-vision task that detects the spatial positions of body, hand, or facial keypoints (joints) from images or video — enabling motion analysis, gesture control, and activity recognition.",
    fullDescription: "Human pose estimation locates anatomical keypoints (joints such as shoulders, elbows, knees) and connects them into a skeleton, for one person (single-person) or many (multi-person). It can be 2D (image-plane coordinates) or 3D (depth-aware), and extends to hand and facial landmark detection.\n\nKey systems and approaches: OpenPose (CMU) popularised real-time multi-person 2D estimation using Part Affinity Fields to associate keypoints across people (a bottom-up approach). Google's MediaPipe provides lightweight, on-device pipelines (Pose, Hands, Face Mesh) optimised for mobile and web. Top-down methods (e.g., HRNet) first detect people then estimate each person's pose, generally more accurate but slower with many subjects. Transformer-based estimators and 3D lifters (VideoPose3D) push accuracy and 3D recovery.\n\nBusiness applications: physiotherapy and rehabilitation (movement quality scoring), sports performance analytics, fitness apps (rep counting, form correction), ergonomics and workplace-safety monitoring (RULA/REBA posture risk), retail and AR (virtual try-on, gesture interfaces), and security (fall detection for elderly care).\n\nUK context: relevant to NHS and private physiotherapy (remote rehab assessment), elderly-care fall detection (a major UK social-care priority), workplace ergonomics compliance under HSE guidance, and sports science. Codeworth builds privacy-conscious pose pipelines (often on-device with MediaPipe to keep video local) for healthcare, fitness, and safety clients, with skeleton-only data retention to minimise personal-data exposure.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "A computer-vision task that detects the spatial positions of body, hand, or facial keypoints (joints) from images or video — enabling motion analysis, gesture control, and activity recognition.",
