    slug: "object-tracking-ml",
    termUk: "Трекінг об'єктів (SORT/DeepSORT)",
    termEn: "Object Tracking (SORT/DeepSORT)",
    category: "ai",
    shortDescription: "A computer-vision task that follows detected objects across consecutive video frames, maintaining a consistent identity for each — implemented by algorithms such as SORT and DeepSORT.",
    fullDescription: "Multi-object tracking (MOT) links object detections across video frames so each object keeps a stable identity over time, answering not just 'what is in this frame' but 'is this the same object as before, and where is it going'. It is the foundation of trajectory analysis, counting, and behaviour understanding in video.\n\nKey algorithms: SORT (Simple Online and Realtime Tracking) combines a Kalman filter (motion prediction) with the Hungarian algorithm (associating detections to existing tracks by IoU) for fast tracking-by-detection. DeepSORT augments SORT with a deep appearance embedding (a re-identification network), dramatically reducing identity switches when objects occlude one another or cross paths. Modern successors include ByteTrack (which recovers low-confidence detections) and transformer-based trackers. Detection quality (YOLO, etc.) heavily influences tracking accuracy.\n\nEvaluation: metrics include MOTA (Multiple Object Tracking Accuracy), IDF1 (identity-preservation), and the number of identity switches.\n\nBusiness applications: retail footfall and queue analytics, people-counting and crowd management, vehicle tracking for traffic and parking analytics, sports analytics (player tracking), and logistics (parcel and asset tracking on conveyors).\n\nUK context: retail footfall analytics (GDPR-conscious, often anonymised counts), smart-city traffic management, stadium crowd safety, and warehouse automation. Codeworth builds DeepSORT/ByteTrack pipelines that produce aggregated, privacy-preserving analytics (counts, dwell times, flow heatmaps) rather than retaining identifiable footage, aligning with ICO surveillance-camera guidance.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "A computer-vision task that follows detected objects across consecutive video frames, maintaining a consistent identity for each — implemented by algorithms such as SORT and DeepSORT.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "Multi-object tracking (MOT) links object detections across video frames so each object keeps a stable identity over time, answering not just 'what is in this frame' but 'is this the same object as before, and where is it going'. It is the foundation of trajectory analysis, counting, and behaviour understanding in video.\\n\\nKey algorithms: SORT (Simple Online and Realtime Tracking) combines a Kalman filter (motion prediction) with the Hungarian algorithm (associating detections to existing tracks by IoU) for fast tracking-by-detection. DeepSORT augments SORT with a deep appearance embedding (a re-identification network), dramatically reducing identity switches when objects occlude one another or cross paths. Modern successors include ByteTrack (which recovers low-confidence detections) and transformer-based trackers. Detection quality (YOLO, etc.) heavily influences tracking accuracy.\\n\\nEvaluation: metrics include MOTA (Multiple Object Tracking Accuracy), IDF1 (identity-preservation), and the number of identity switches.\\n\\nBusiness applications: retail footfall and queue analytics, people-counting and crowd management, vehicle tracking for traffic and parking analytics, sports analytics (player tracking), and logistics (parcel and asset tracking on conveyors).\\n\\nUK context: retail footfall analytics (GDPR-conscious, often anonymised counts), smart-city traffic management, stadium crowd safety, and warehouse automation. Codeworth builds DeepSORT/ByteTrack pipelines that produce aggregated, privacy-preserving analytics (counts, dwell times, flow heatmaps) rather than retaining identifiable footage, aligning with ICO surveillance-camera guidance.",
