    slug: "depth-estimation-ml",
    termUk: "Оцінка глибини (монокулярна для AR)",
    termEn: "Monocular Depth Estimation (for AR)",
    category: "ai",
    shortDescription: "A computer-vision task that infers per-pixel distance (depth) from images — especially monocular depth estimation from a single camera — enabling augmented reality, robotics, and 3D scene understanding.",
    fullDescription: "Depth estimation recovers the distance from the camera to each point in a scene. Stereo and LiDAR provide depth directly, but monocular depth estimation infers it from a single ordinary camera image using learned priors — a far cheaper and more deployable approach, central to mobile AR.\n\nKey approaches: Supervised models train on ground-truth depth (from LiDAR/RGB-D). Self-supervised methods (e.g., Monodepth2) learn depth from monocular video or stereo pairs using photometric reprojection loss, avoiding costly depth labels. Recent foundation models — MiDaS (robust relative depth across diverse scenes) and Depth Anything / ZoeDepth (zero-shot metric depth) — generalise strongly to new environments. Outputs may be relative (ordering) or metric (true distances).\n\nWhy it matters for AR and robotics: depth enables correct occlusion (virtual objects hidden behind real ones), realistic placement and scaling of AR content, collision avoidance, and 3D reconstruction — all from a phone camera without specialised sensors.\n\nBusiness applications: AR retail (furniture and product placement, virtual try-on with correct scale), measurement apps (room and object dimensions), robotics and drones (obstacle avoidance), and construction/surveying (rapid 3D capture).\n\nUK context: AR commerce for retailers and estate agents (virtual staging, property measurement), construction-tech 3D site capture, and warehouse robotics. Codeworth integrates foundation depth models (Depth Anything / MiDaS) into mobile AR experiences and measurement tools, calibrating to metric scale where accurate dimensions are required.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "A computer-vision task that infers per-pixel distance (depth) from images — especially monocular depth estimation from a single camera — enabling augmented reality, robotics, and 3D scene understanding.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "Depth estimation recovers the distance from the camera to each point in a scene. Stereo and LiDAR provide depth directly, but monocular depth estimation infers it from a single ordinary camera image using learned priors — a far cheaper and more deployable approach, central to mobile AR.\\n\\nKey approaches: Supervised models train on ground-truth depth (from LiDAR/RGB-D). Self-supervised methods (e.g., Monodepth2) learn depth from monocular video or stereo pairs using photometric reprojection loss, avoiding costly depth labels. Recent foundation models — MiDaS (robust relative depth across diverse scenes) and Depth Anything / ZoeDepth (zero-shot metric depth) — generalise strongly to new environments. Outputs may be relative (ordering) or metric (true distances).\\n\\nWhy it matters for AR and robotics: depth enables correct occlusion (virtual objects hidden behind real ones), realistic placement and scaling of AR content, collision avoidance, and 3D reconstruction — all from a phone camera without specialised sensors.\\n\\nBusiness applications: AR retail (furniture and product placement, virtual try-on with correct scale), measurement apps (room and object dimensions), robotics and drones (obstacle avoidance), and construction/surveying (rapid 3D capture).\\n\\nUK context: AR commerce for retailers and estate agents (virtual staging, property measurement), construction-tech 3D site capture, and warehouse robotics. Codeworth integrates foundation depth models (Depth Anything / MiDaS) into mobile AR experiences and measurement tools, calibrating to metric scale where accurate dimensions are required.",
    exampleEn: "A UK furniture retailer's AR app uses monocular depth estimation so a virtual sofa is correctly occluded by a real coffee table and scaled to the room, letting customers see true fit before purchase and reducing returns.",
