    slug: "tick-data-ml",
    termUk: "ML на тік-даних (HFT)",
    termEn: "Tick Data Machine Learning (HFT)",
    category: "data",
    shortDescription: "Machine learning applied to high-frequency tick-by-tick market data — every trade and quote — for microstructure modelling, short-horizon prediction, and execution optimisation.",
    fullDescription: "Tick data is the most granular financial time series: a record of every trade and every quote update, timestamped to microseconds or finer, often including full limit-order-book depth. High-frequency trading (HFT) and electronic execution rely on extracting signal from this firehose, where the relevant patterns play out over milliseconds to seconds.\n\nDistinctive challenges: tick data is irregularly spaced (events, not fixed intervals), enormous in volume (terabytes per day), highly noisy, and subject to microstructure effects (bid-ask bounce, discreteness, latency). Standard time-series tooling assumes regular sampling, so practitioners use event-based bars (tick, volume, or dollar bars) and microstructure features (order-flow imbalance, depth, spread, trade signs).\n\nML methods: order-flow imbalance and limit-order-book features feed gradient-boosted trees and deep models (LSTMs, temporal CNNs, and deep order-book models like DeepLOB) to predict short-horizon price moves or fill probabilities. Reinforcement learning is used for optimal execution (minimising market impact when working a large order). Rigorous backtesting must account for latency, transaction costs, and the danger of look-ahead bias.\n\nUK context: London is a global trading hub; applications span market-making, smart order routing, and best-execution analytics under FCA/MiFID II obligations (firms must evidence best execution). Codeworth builds microstructure feature pipelines and short-horizon predictive models, with a strong emphasis on leakage-free backtesting and realistic cost/latency modelling so results survive contact with live markets.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "Machine learning applied to high-frequency tick-by-tick market data — every trade and quote — for microstructure modelling, short-horizon prediction, and execution optimisation.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "Tick data is the most granular financial time series: a record of every trade and every quote update, timestamped to microseconds or finer, often including full limit-order-book depth. High-frequency trading (HFT) and electronic execution rely on extracting signal from this firehose, where the relevant patterns play out over milliseconds to seconds.\\n\\nDistinctive challenges: tick data is irregularly spaced (events, not fixed intervals), enormous in volume (terabytes per day), highly noisy, and subject to microstructure effects (bid-ask bounce, discreteness, latency). Standard time-series tooling assumes regular sampling, so practitioners use event-based bars (tick, volume, or dollar bars) and microstructure features (order-flow imbalance, depth, spread, trade signs).\\n\\nML methods: order-flow imbalance and limit-order-book features feed gradient-boosted trees and deep models (LSTMs, temporal CNNs, and deep order-book models like DeepLOB) to predict short-horizon price moves or fill probabilities. Reinforcement learning is used for optimal execution (minimising market impact when working a large order). Rigorous backtesting must account for latency, transaction costs, and the danger of look-ahead bias.\\n\\nUK context: London is a global trading hub; applications span market-making, smart order routing, and best-execution analytics under FCA/MiFID II obligations (firms must evidence best execution). Codeworth builds microstructure feature pipelines and short-horizon predictive models, with a strong emphasis on leakage-free backtesting and realistic cost/latency modelling so results survive contact with live markets.",
