    slug: "nowcasting-ml",
    termUk: "ML-нау-кастинг (економічний)",
    termEn: "Economic Nowcasting with ML",
    category: "data",
    shortDescription: "The use of machine learning to estimate the current or very-near-term state of the economy (e.g., this quarter's GDP) before official statistics are released, by fusing timely high-frequency indicators.",
    fullDescription: "Nowcasting estimates the present and immediate future — for example, current-quarter GDP growth — before official figures arrive, which are typically published with substantial lags and later revised. The core idea is to exploit a wide range of higher-frequency, more timely indicators (industrial output, retail sales, surveys, card spending, traffic, energy use, even satellite and web data) to infer the slow-moving target as new data trickle in.\n\nKey challenges and methods: indicators arrive at mixed frequencies (daily, weekly, monthly) and with a ragged edge (different publication lags), and there are far more candidate predictors than observations. Classical approaches use dynamic factor models and the Kalman filter (extracting common factors from many series); ML approaches add regularised regression (LASSO/elastic net) for variable selection, gradient boosting, random forests, and neural networks to capture non-linearities, often combined with MIDAS (mixed-data sampling) regressions to handle frequency mismatch. Real-time vintage data (avoiding look-ahead from revisions) is essential for honest evaluation.\n\nWhy it matters for business: timely reads on demand and the macro environment inform inventory, hiring, pricing, and treasury decisions weeks ahead of official releases.\n\nUK context: the Bank of England, ONS, and the NIESR (which publishes a monthly GDP tracker) actively use nowcasting; firms use it for demand-environment monitoring. Codeworth builds nowcasting models that ingest alternative and high-frequency UK data (card-spend proxies, ONS faster indicators) with regularisation and proper real-time backtesting to support planning decisions.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "The use of machine learning to estimate the current or very-near-term state of the economy (e.g., this quarter's GDP) before official statistics are released, by fusing timely high-frequency indicators.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "Nowcasting estimates the present and immediate future — for example, current-quarter GDP growth — before official figures arrive, which are typically published with substantial lags and later revised. The core idea is to exploit a wide range of higher-frequency, more timely indicators (industrial output, retail sales, surveys, card spending, traffic, energy use, even satellite and web data) to infer the slow-moving target as new data trickle in.\\n\\nKey challenges and methods: indicators arrive at mixed frequencies (daily, weekly, monthly) and with a ragged edge (different publication lags), and there are far more candidate predictors than observations. Classical approaches use dynamic factor models and the Kalman filter (extracting common factors from many series); ML approaches add regularised regression (LASSO/elastic net) for variable selection, gradient boosting, random forests, and neural networks to capture non-linearities, often combined with MIDAS (mixed-data sampling) regressions to handle frequency mismatch. Real-time vintage data (avoiding look-ahead from revisions) is essential for honest evaluation.\\n\\nWhy it matters for business: timely reads on demand and the macro environment inform inventory, hiring, pricing, and treasury decisions weeks ahead of official releases.\\n\\nUK context: the Bank of England, ONS, and the NIESR (which publishes a monthly GDP tracker) actively use nowcasting; firms use it for demand-environment monitoring. Codeworth builds nowcasting models that ingest alternative and high-frequency UK data (card-spend proxies, ONS faster indicators) with regularisation and proper real-time backtesting to support planning decisions.",
