    slug: "multivariate-time-series",
    termUk: "Багатовимірні часові ряди (TFT/N-HiTS)",
    termEn: "Multivariate Time Series (TFT/N-HiTS)",
    category: "data",
    shortDescription: "Forecasting multiple interrelated time series jointly, including exogenous drivers, using modern deep architectures such as the Temporal Fusion Transformer (TFT) and N-HiTS.",
    fullDescription: "Multivariate time-series forecasting models several series and their interdependencies together, optionally with exogenous (known-future) and static covariates, rather than predicting each series in isolation. Global models — trained across many related series — learn shared patterns and generalise far better than fitting a separate univariate model per series, especially for large catalogues (thousands of SKUs, stores, or sensors).\n\nKey architectures: the Temporal Fusion Transformer (TFT, Google 2019) combines LSTM encoders with attention, handles static / time-varying-known / time-varying-unknown inputs explicitly, supports quantile (probabilistic) forecasts, and offers interpretability via variable-selection networks and attention weights. N-HiTS (2022) extends N-BEATS with hierarchical, multi-rate interpolation for accurate and fast long-horizon forecasting. Other strong options include DeepAR (probabilistic autoregressive), Informer/Autoformer (long-sequence transformers), and PatchTST. Libraries such as Darts, GluonTS, and PyTorch Forecasting make these accessible.\n\nWhy it matters: probabilistic, multi-horizon forecasts with quantiles enable inventory and capacity decisions based on risk (e.g., stock to the 90th percentile of demand), and global models share learning across the long tail of sparse series.\n\nUK context: retail and grocery demand across thousands of SKUs and stores, energy demand across regions, and supply-chain planning. Codeworth deploys TFT and N-HiTS global models that produce quantile forecasts and interpretable driver attributions, giving UK operations teams both accuracy and the explanation of which factors (promotions, weather, seasonality) drive each forecast.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "Forecasting multiple interrelated time series jointly, including exogenous drivers, using modern deep architectures such as the Temporal Fusion Transformer (TFT) and N-HiTS.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "Multivariate time-series forecasting models several series and their interdependencies together, optionally with exogenous (known-future) and static covariates, rather than predicting each series in isolation. Global models — trained across many related series — learn shared patterns and generalise far better than fitting a separate univariate model per series, especially for large catalogues (thousands of SKUs, stores, or sensors).\\n\\nKey architectures: the Temporal Fusion Transformer (TFT, Google 2019) combines LSTM encoders with attention, handles static / time-varying-known / time-varying-unknown inputs explicitly, supports quantile (probabilistic) forecasts, and offers interpretability via variable-selection networks and attention weights. N-HiTS (2022) extends N-BEATS with hierarchical, multi-rate interpolation for accurate and fast long-horizon forecasting. Other strong options include DeepAR (probabilistic autoregressive), Informer/Autoformer (long-sequence transformers), and PatchTST. Libraries such as Darts, GluonTS, and PyTorch Forecasting make these accessible.\\n\\nWhy it matters: probabilistic, multi-horizon forecasts with quantiles enable inventory and capacity decisions based on risk (e.g., stock to the 90th percentile of demand), and global models share learning across the long tail of sparse series.\\n\\nUK context: retail and grocery demand across thousands of SKUs and stores, energy demand across regions, and supply-chain planning. Codeworth deploys TFT and N-HiTS global models that produce quantile forecasts and interpretable driver attributions, giving UK operations teams both accuracy and the explanation of which factors (promotions, weather, seasonality) drive each forecast.",
