    slug: "ml-pipeline-orchestration",
    termUk: "Оркестрація ML-конвеєрів (Airflow/Prefect/Kedro)",
    termEn: "ML Pipeline Orchestration (Airflow/Prefect/Kedro)",
    category: "mlops",
    shortDescription: "The coordination of multi-step machine learning workflows — ingestion, processing, training, evaluation, deployment — as reliable, scheduled, observable pipelines using tools like Airflow, Prefect, and Kedro.",
    fullDescription: "ML pipeline orchestration turns a tangle of ad-hoc scripts into a managed, repeatable workflow with explicit task dependencies, scheduling, retries, logging, and monitoring. A typical ML pipeline chains data ingestion, validation, feature engineering, training, evaluation, registration, and deployment — and each step must run in order, recover from failures, and be observable in production.\n\nKey tools: Apache Airflow is the long-standing standard, defining workflows as Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) in Python with a rich scheduler and UI; it excels at scheduled batch orchestration. Prefect offers a more Pythonic, dynamic flow model with strong handling of failures, retries, and modern deployment patterns. Kedro (from QuantumBlack/McKinsey) focuses on reproducible, modular data-science project structure — pipelines, data catalogues, and software-engineering best practices — and integrates with Airflow/Prefect for execution. ML-native options include Kubeflow Pipelines, Metaflow, Dagster, and ZenML.\n\nWhy it matters: orchestration provides reproducibility (the same DAG runs identically), reliability (automatic retries, alerting), scheduling (nightly retrains), lineage (what produced this model), and scalability (parallel/distributed tasks). It is the backbone of production MLOps and a prerequisite for trustworthy automated retraining.\n\nUK context: regulated firms need auditable, reproducible pipelines to evidence model governance to the FCA/PRA (who produced which model from which data, when). Codeworth builds orchestrated pipelines (commonly Airflow or Prefect, with Kedro for project structure) that log lineage and integrate with model registries and monitoring for fully auditable ML operations.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "The coordination of multi-step machine learning workflows — ingestion, processing, training, evaluation, deployment — as reliable, scheduled, observable pipelines using tools like Airflow, Prefect, and Kedro.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "ML pipeline orchestration turns a tangle of ad-hoc scripts into a managed, repeatable workflow with explicit task dependencies, scheduling, retries, logging, and monitoring. A typical ML pipeline chains data ingestion, validation, feature engineering, training, evaluation, registration, and deployment — and each step must run in order, recover from failures, and be observable in production.\\n\\nKey tools: Apache Airflow is the long-standing standard, defining workflows as Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) in Python with a rich scheduler and UI; it excels at scheduled batch orchestration. Prefect offers a more Pythonic, dynamic flow model with strong handling of failures, retries, and modern deployment patterns. Kedro (from QuantumBlack/McKinsey) focuses on reproducible, modular data-science project structure — pipelines, data catalogues, and software-engineering best practices — and integrates with Airflow/Prefect for execution. ML-native options include Kubeflow Pipelines, Metaflow, Dagster, and ZenML.\\n\\nWhy it matters: orchestration provides reproducibility (the same DAG runs identically), reliability (automatic retries, alerting), scheduling (nightly retrains), lineage (what produced this model), and scalability (parallel/distributed tasks). It is the backbone of production MLOps and a prerequisite for trustworthy automated retraining.\\n\\nUK context: regulated firms need auditable, reproducible pipelines to evidence model governance to the FCA/PRA (who produced which model from which data, when). Codeworth builds orchestrated pipelines (commonly Airflow or Prefect, with Kedro for project structure) that log lineage and integrate with model registries and monitoring for fully auditable ML operations.",
