    category: "mlops",
    shortDescription: "The optimisation of inference latency when serving machine learning models in production, using runtimes and compilers such as ONNX Runtime and NVIDIA TensorRT.",
    fullDescription: "Model serving latency is the time from receiving an inference request to returning a prediction. In production, latency directly affects user experience and feasibility: a fraud check at checkout, a real-time recommendation, or an LLM response must meet tight latency budgets (often tens of milliseconds), and high latency at scale also drives cost.\n\nKey optimisation techniques: (1) Graph/runtime optimisation — ONNX (Open Neural Network Exchange) provides a portable model format, and ONNX Runtime applies graph fusions and hardware-specific execution providers for fast cross-framework inference. (2) Compilation and kernel fusion — NVIDIA TensorRT compiles and optimises models for specific GPUs, fusing layers and selecting optimal kernels. (3) Quantisation — reducing precision (FP32 to FP16 or INT8) for faster, smaller models with minimal accuracy loss. (4) Pruning and distillation — shrinking models. (5) Dynamic batching — grouping concurrent requests to improve throughput. (6) Hardware choice — GPU vs CPU vs specialised accelerators, and edge deployment.\n\nThe trade-off: latency, throughput, accuracy, and cost must be balanced. Aggressive quantisation cuts latency and cost but must be validated for accuracy; batching improves throughput at the expense of per-request latency. Measuring tail latency (p95, p99), not just the average, is essential because the slowest requests define user experience.\n\nUK context: latency-sensitive use cases include payments fraud screening, real-time pricing, and customer-facing LLMs, where data-residency may also pin inference to UK regions. Codeworth optimises serving with ONNX Runtime / TensorRT, quantisation, and dynamic batching, tuning to meet agreed p99 latency SLAs while controlling GPU cost.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "The optimisation of inference latency when serving machine learning models in production, using runtimes and compilers such as ONNX Runtime and NVIDIA TensorRT.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "Model serving latency is the time from receiving an inference request to returning a prediction. In production, latency directly affects user experience and feasibility: a fraud check at checkout, a real-time recommendation, or an LLM response must meet tight latency budgets (often tens of milliseconds), and high latency at scale also drives cost.\\n\\nKey optimisation techniques: (1) Graph/runtime optimisation — ONNX (Open Neural Network Exchange) provides a portable model format, and ONNX Runtime applies graph fusions and hardware-specific execution providers for fast cross-framework inference. (2) Compilation and kernel fusion — NVIDIA TensorRT compiles and optimises models for specific GPUs, fusing layers and selecting optimal kernels. (3) Quantisation — reducing precision (FP32 to FP16 or INT8) for faster, smaller models with minimal accuracy loss. (4) Pruning and distillation — shrinking models. (5) Dynamic batching — grouping concurrent requests to improve throughput. (6) Hardware choice — GPU vs CPU vs specialised accelerators, and edge deployment.\\n\\nThe trade-off: latency, throughput, accuracy, and cost must be balanced. Aggressive quantisation cuts latency and cost but must be validated for accuracy; batching improves throughput at the expense of per-request latency. Measuring tail latency (p95, p99), not just the average, is essential because the slowest requests define user experience.\\n\\nUK context: latency-sensitive use cases include payments fraud screening, real-time pricing, and customer-facing LLMs, where data-residency may also pin inference to UK regions. Codeworth optimises serving with ONNX Runtime / TensorRT, quantisation, and dynamic batching, tuning to meet agreed p99 latency SLAs while controlling GPU cost.",
