    slug: "ml-cost-optimization",
    termUk: "Оптимізація витрат на ML (spot/preemptible)",
    termEn: "ML Cost Optimisation (Spot/Preemptible)",
    category: "infrastructure",
    shortDescription: "Techniques for reducing the cloud cost of machine learning workloads — notably using spot/preemptible instances, autoscaling, and right-sizing — without compromising reliability.",
    fullDescription: "ML workloads, especially GPU-heavy training and high-throughput inference, are among the most expensive items in a cloud bill. ML cost optimisation (FinOps for ML) systematically reduces this spend while preserving performance and reliability.\n\nKey levers: (1) Spot / preemptible instances — deeply discounted (often 60-90% cheaper) spare cloud capacity that the provider can reclaim with little notice (AWS Spot, GCP Preemptible/Spot VMs, Azure Spot). Ideal for fault-tolerant training with checkpointing and for stateless batch inference; combine with checkpoint/resume and graceful interruption handling so reclaimed nodes do not lose work. (2) Autoscaling and scale-to-zero — serving infrastructure (e.g., KServe) that scales down when idle, so you pay only for actual demand. (3) Right-sizing and GPU sharing — matching instance type/size to the workload, using fractional GPUs (MIG) or multi-model serving to raise utilisation. (4) Quantisation and smaller models — cutting compute per inference. (5) Reserved/committed-use discounts and savings plans for predictable baseline load. (6) Spot diversification across instance types/zones to reduce interruption risk.\n\nWhy it matters: ML budgets can spiral; disciplined cost engineering can cut spend by half or more, freeing budget for more experimentation and making ML projects economically viable.\n\nUK context: UK firms balance cost against data-residency (UK-region pricing) and reliability SLAs. Codeworth architects training on spot instances with automatic checkpointing, scale-to-zero serving, right-sized GPUs, and committed-use discounts for steady workloads — typically cutting client ML cloud spend substantially while meeting reliability targets.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "Techniques for reducing the cloud cost of machine learning workloads — notably using spot/preemptible instances, autoscaling, and right-sizing — without compromising reliability.",
