    slug: "small-language-models",
    termUk: "Малі мовні моделі (SLM)",
    termEn: "Small Language Models (SLMs)",
    category: "ai",
    shortDescription: "Compact language models (roughly 1-13B parameters) designed to run on-device or on modest cloud hardware, trading some general capability for lower latency, lower cost, and data-residency control.",
    fullDescription: "Small Language Models (SLMs) — models such as Phi-3, Gemma, Llama 3 8B, or Mistral 7B — are trained to pack strong task performance into a fraction of the parameters of frontier LLMs (GPT-4 class, Claude Opus class). Where a frontier LLM chases broad, open-ended reasoning across every domain, an SLM is usually fine-tuned or distilled for a narrower set of tasks (classification, extraction, domain chat, code completion) and optimised to run on a single GPU, a laptop CPU, or even a phone.\n\nThe business case is cost and latency, not just novelty. Frontier LLM API calls charge per token and route over the public internet, adding both spend and round-trip latency; an SLM served on-premises or at the edge removes both. For UK businesses handling regulated data (financial transactions, NHS patient notes, legal documents), an SLM that runs entirely within a private VPC or on-device sidesteps the question of whether data ever left UK jurisdiction — a question a third-party LLM API cannot avoid raising.\n\nThe trade-off is capability: SLMs lag frontier LLMs on open-ended reasoning, broad world knowledge, and long-context tasks. The practical pattern Codeworth applies is triage — route routine, well-scoped requests (intent classification, document field extraction, FAQ answering) to a fine-tuned SLM running on cost-efficient infrastructure, and escalate genuinely hard or ambiguous requests to a frontier LLM API. This cuts blended inference cost significantly while keeping quality where it matters.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "Compact language models (roughly 1-13B parameters) designed to run on-device or on modest cloud hardware, trading some general capability for lower latency, lower cost, and data-residency control.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "Small Language Models (SLMs) — models such as Phi-3, Gemma, Llama 3 8B, or Mistral 7B — are trained to pack strong task performance into a fraction of the parameters of frontier LLMs (GPT-4 class, Claude Opus class). Where a frontier LLM chases broad, open-ended reasoning across every domain, an SLM is usually fine-tuned or distilled for a narrower set of tasks (classification, extraction, domain chat, code completion) and optimised to run on a single GPU, a laptop CPU, or even a phone.\\n\\nThe business case is cost and latency, not just novelty. Frontier LLM API calls charge per token and route over the public internet, adding both spend and round-trip latency; an SLM served on-premises or at the edge removes both. For UK businesses handling regulated data (financial transactions, NHS patient notes, legal documents), an SLM that runs entirely within a private VPC or on-device sidesteps the question of whether data ever left UK jurisdiction — a question a third-party LLM API cannot avoid raising.\\n\\nThe trade-off is capability: SLMs lag frontier LLMs on open-ended reasoning, broad world knowledge, and long-context tasks. The practical pattern Codeworth applies is triage — route routine, well-scoped requests (intent classification, document field extraction, FAQ answering) to a fine-tuned SLM running on cost-efficient infrastructure, and escalate genuinely hard or ambiguous requests to a frontier LLM API. This cuts blended inference cost significantly while keeping quality where it matters.",
    exampleEn: "A UK insurer runs a fine-tuned 8B-parameter SLM on its own GPU server to triage and extract fields from incoming claims emails — no data leaves the building and inference cost drops to a fraction of routing every email through a frontier LLM API — while genuinely ambiguous claims are escalated to GPT-4/Claude for a full read.",
