    slug: "world-models",
    termUk: "Моделі світу (World Models)",
    termEn: "AI World Models",
    category: "ai",
    shortDescription: "AI systems that learn an internal, predictive simulation of how an environment behaves over time (as in Sora, Genie, or Tesla's occupancy networks), enabling planning and 'what-if' rollouts rather than single-step prediction.",
    fullDescription: "A world model is a learned internal representation of how a physical or virtual environment evolves — given the current state and an action, it predicts the next state. This is distinct from a generic generative model that produces a single plausible output: a world model is built to be rolled forward step by step, so an agent (robot, game character, self-driving car, planning system) can imagine several possible futures before acting, rather than reacting one step at a time.\n\nRecent consumer-visible examples include video-generation systems (OpenAI Sora, Google Veo) and interactive world simulators (DeepMind Genie) that generate coherent, controllable video sequences long enough to look like a learned physics and object-permanence simulation. Under the hood, these differ from earlier reinforcement-learning world models (used in robotics and autonomous driving for years) mainly in scale and visual fidelity, not in the core idea: predict forward, then plan against the prediction.\n\nFor UK businesses this is currently more R&D horizon than off-the-shelf service, but it is directly relevant to three areas Codeworth tracks for clients: warehouse and logistics robotics (simulating a robot's next moves before committing), synthetic training data generation (simulating rare scenarios — a factory floor near-miss, a rare fraud pattern — that are too dangerous or too rare to collect real data for), and manufacturing digital twins where a learned model, not a hand-built physics engine, predicts how a production line will behave under a proposed change.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "AI systems that learn an internal, predictive simulation of how an environment behaves over time (as in Sora, Genie, or Tesla's occupancy networks), enabling planning and 'what-if' rollouts rather than single-step prediction.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "A world model is a learned internal representation of how a physical or virtual environment evolves — given the current state and an action, it predicts the next state. This is distinct from a generic generative model that produces a single plausible output: a world model is built to be rolled forward step by step, so an agent (robot, game character, self-driving car, planning system) can imagine several possible futures before acting, rather than reacting one step at a time.\\n\\nRecent consumer-visible examples include video-generation systems (OpenAI Sora, Google Veo) and interactive world simulators (DeepMind Genie) that generate coherent, controllable video sequences long enough to look like a learned physics and object-permanence simulation. Under the hood, these differ from earlier reinforcement-learning world models (used in robotics and autonomous driving for years) mainly in scale and visual fidelity, not in the core idea: predict forward, then plan against the prediction.\\n\\nFor UK businesses this is currently more R&D horizon than off-the-shelf service, but it is directly relevant to three areas Codeworth tracks for clients: warehouse and logistics robotics (simulating a robot's next moves before committing), synthetic training data generation (simulating rare scenarios — a factory floor near-miss, a rare fraud pattern — that are too dangerous or too rare to collect real data for), and manufacturing digital twins where a learned model, not a hand-built physics engine, predicts how a production line will behave under a proposed change.",
    exampleEn: "A UK logistics operator prototypes a warehouse-robot path-planning system using a world model trained on depot camera footage: before committing to a route, the robot 'imagines' several candidate paths through the model and picks the one predicted to avoid congestion, rather than replanning reactively after each collision risk.",
