    slug: "context-caching-llm",
    termUk: "Кешування контексту LLM (Context Caching)",
    termEn: "LLM Context Caching",
    category: "ai",
    shortDescription: "A provider-side pricing and performance feature that caches a previously-processed prompt prefix (a long system prompt, document, or conversation history) so repeated calls reusing it are cheaper and faster — distinct from a model's internal KV-cache mechanics.",
    fullDescription: "LLM context caching (offered by Anthropic as prompt caching, and by other providers under similar names) lets an application mark a portion of its prompt — typically a long, unchanging prefix such as a system prompt, a set of few-shot examples, or a large reference document — as cacheable. On the first call, the provider processes and caches that prefix; on subsequent calls that reuse the identical prefix, the provider skips reprocessing it and charges a substantially reduced rate (often a small fraction of the standard input-token price) for the cached portion, while only the new, variable part of the prompt is billed at full rate.\n\nThis is a distinct concept from the KV-cache that operates purely inside a transformer's internal inference mechanics during a single generation pass (caching key/value tensors token-by-token so each new token doesn't require reprocessing the whole sequence) — context caching, as a product feature, persists a cache across separate API calls (often for minutes), and is something an application developer explicitly designs for, not an automatic internal optimisation invisible to the caller. Getting the benefit requires structuring prompts so the large, reusable portion is a stable prefix and the small, per-request-specific portion (the actual user question) comes last, since the cache only helps for an exact-matching prefix.\n\nFor UK businesses running any LLM application with a long, repeated system prompt or a large shared reference document (a product catalogue, a company knowledge base, a legal template library) across many requests, context caching is one of the highest-leverage, lowest-effort cost optimisations available — often cutting the dominant cost driver (repeatedly re-sending a large fixed prefix) by 80-90% with a prompt-structure change rather than an architecture change.",
    shortDescriptionEn: "A provider-side pricing and performance feature that caches a previously-processed prompt prefix (a long system prompt, document, or conversation history) so repeated calls reusing it are cheaper and faster — distinct from a model's internal KV-cache mechanics.",
    fullDescriptionEn: "LLM context caching (offered by Anthropic as prompt caching, and by other providers under similar names) lets an application mark a portion of its prompt — typically a long, unchanging prefix such as a system prompt, a set of few-shot examples, or a large reference document — as cacheable. On the first call, the provider processes and caches that prefix; on subsequent calls that reuse the identical prefix, the provider skips reprocessing it and charges a substantially reduced rate (often a small fraction of the standard input-token price) for the cached portion, while only the new, variable part of the prompt is billed at full rate.\\n\\nThis is a distinct concept from the KV-cache that operates purely inside a transformer's internal inference mechanics during a single generation pass (caching key/value tensors token-by-token so each new token doesn't require reprocessing the whole sequence) — context caching, as a product feature, persists a cache across separate API calls (often for minutes), and is something an application developer explicitly designs for, not an automatic internal optimisation invisible to the caller. Getting the benefit requires structuring prompts so the large, reusable portion is a stable prefix and the small, per-request-specific portion (the actual user question) comes last, since the cache only helps for an exact-matching prefix.\\n\\nFor UK businesses running any LLM application with a long, repeated system prompt or a large shared reference document (a product catalogue, a company knowledge base, a legal template library) across many requests, context caching is one of the highest-leverage, lowest-effort cost optimisations available — often cutting the dominant cost driver (repeatedly re-sending a large fixed prefix) by 80-90% with a prompt-structure change rather than an architecture change.",
    exampleEn: "Codeworth restructures a UK legal-tech client's contract-review prompt so its 4,000-token instructions-and-clause-library prefix is marked cacheable and only the specific contract text varies per call; with thousands of daily reviews reusing the same prefix, context caching cuts the client's LLM API bill by roughly 70% with no change to the underlying model or feature.",
    example: "Codeworth restructures a UK legal-tech client's contract-review prompt so its 4,000-token instructions-and-clause-library prefix is marked cacheable and only the specific contract text varies per call; with thousands of daily reviews reusing the same prefix, context caching cuts the client's LLM API bill by roughly 70% with no change to the underlying model or feature.",
